  <p><strong>MarketTriggers.com explains why U.S. financial markets move.</strong> Plenty of sites report what happened in the market today. We focus on the part most coverage skips: the specific economic data, Federal Reserve decisions, corporate events, and sector dynamics behind each move, and what investors should watch next.</p>

  <h2>Our mission</h2>
  <p>We want every reader to be able to look at a market headline and answer four questions: <em>What happened? Why does it matter? How could it affect the market? What should I watch next?</em> Each article we publish is built around those questions.</p>
  <p>Our readers are individual investors, students, and professionals who want to understand financial markets more clearly — without hype, jargon, or sales pitches.</p>

  <h2>What we cover</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/economic-indicators/">U.S. economic indicators</a> — inflation, employment, GDP, retail sales, consumer confidence, and Treasury yields</li>
    <li><a href="/stock-market-triggers/">Stock market triggers</a> — earnings, guidance, analyst actions, buybacks, dividends, and IPOs</li>
    <li><a href="/market-sectors/">Market sectors</a> — how different industries respond to the same news</li>
    <li><a href="/investor-education/">Investor education</a> — how markets work, from volatility to risk management</li>
    <li><a href="/market-events/">Market events</a> — previews of upcoming releases and policy meetings</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Editorial standards</h2>
  <p>Financial information can influence real decisions, so we follow clear standards on every page:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Primary sources first.</strong> We cite official sources such as the Bureau of Labor Statistics, Bureau of Economic Analysis, Census Bureau, Federal Reserve, U.S. Treasury, and SEC filings wherever possible.</li>
    <li><strong>Accuracy over speed.</strong> We do not publish statistics, quotations, or data we cannot verify.</li>
    <li><strong>Clear labeling.</strong> We separate historical facts, analysis, and hypothetical scenarios so readers know which is which.</li>
    <li><strong>Corrections and updates.</strong> When information changes or we find an error, we update the article and show the date it was last modified.</li>
    <li><strong>Independence.</strong> Our explanations are not paid for by companies we write about.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Read the full <a href="/editorial-policy/">Editorial Policy</a>.</p>

  <h2>Not financial advice</h2>
  <p>MarketTriggers.com is an educational publication. We do not provide personalized investment, financial, tax, or legal advice, and nothing on this site is a recommendation to buy or sell any security. For decisions about your own money, consult a qualified, licensed professional.</p>

  <h2>Advertising</h2>
  <p>We may display advertising to support the site. Advertising never influences our editorial content, and ads are clearly separated from articles. See our <a href="/privacy-policy/">Privacy Policy</a> for details on how advertising partners may use cookies.</p>

  <h2>Contact us</h2>
  <p>Questions, corrections, or feedback? Visit our <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> or email <a href="mailto:info.christopherkunz@gmail.com">info.christopherkunz@gmail.com</a>.</p>
